id: rust.question-mark
kind: rust
title: The ? operator
aliases: question mark, try operator, propagation
tags: errors, ergonomics
questions:
  - what does the ? operator do and when can you not use it?
invariant: `?` early-returns on Err after applying `From` conversion to the enclosing function's error type.
answer_30s: `?` propagates the error to the caller, converting it into the function's error type through `From`.
answer_2m: |
  Precisely: on Err(e) it returns Err(From::from(e)) from the enclosing
  function, so the return type must admit failure (Result, Option, ControlFlow).
  That is why `?` cannot appear in a function returning a plain value, and why
  error enums work so well with thiserror's `#[from]`: the propagation site
  stays one character while the type system tracks the conversion. When you need
  context, retry, or translation, do that before the `?`.
when_to_use:
  - everywhere in fallible functions; the default propagation style
avoid_when:
  - when the error must be enriched (context), retried, or mapped first
minimal_example: |
  #[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
  enum LoadError {
      #[error("io: {0}")] Io(#[from] std::io::Error),
      #[error("parse: {0}")] Parse(#[from] toml::de::Error),
  }

  fn load(p: &str) -> Result<Config, LoadError> {
      let body = std::fs::read_to_string(p)?; // io -> LoadError::Io
      let cfg = toml::from_str(&body)?;       // parse -> LoadError::Parse
      Ok(cfg)
  }
failure_modes:
  - losing context, so the log says "no such file" with no idea which file
  - using ? in a closure returning a plain value (a compile error easily misread as a borrow issue)
related:
  - rust.result
  - rust.error-enum
  - rust.anyhow-thiserror
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/result/index.html#the-question-mark-operator-
